        <Document ID="51EA1C20-B9EB-4D83-92CE-248B436103D6">
            <Title>Architectural Decision Statement</Title>
            <Text>The Y-Statement is a highly structured, single-sentence template used to capture the core architectural decision quickly. It gets its name because its visual architecture or structural flow resembles the letter "Y"—branching out from a context into a choice, a rationale, and the resulting trade-offs.
It was popularized by software architect Olaf Zimmermann to help teams document decisions in a fast, punchy, and standardized way without writing pages of documentation.

## The Template

In the context of [context],
we faced the challenge of [problem].
We decided on [selected option],
and neglected [alternative options],
to achieve [benefits],
accepting [drawbacks/risks].

## A Concrete Example
Here is how a real-world decision looks when mapped to a Y-Statement:

In the context of our new e-commerce microservices,
we faced the challenge of choosing a reliable data storage for shopping carts.
We decided on Redis,
and neglected PostgreSQL and DynamoDB,
to achieve sub-millisecond read/write speeds and automatic session expiration,
accepting the risk of data loss if the cache node crashes before persistence occurs.

## Why Use It?

* Forces Clarity: Because it is a single sentence, you cannot hide vague reasoning behind paragraphs of text.
* Highly Scannable: Engineers can read 5 different Y-Statements in under a minute to understand a system's entire history.
* Perfect for Git: It fits beautifully into a commit message or a pull request description.

https://share.google/aimode/tOl0xgIz03UJ5vnmt 

</Text>
        </Document>
